 "sentences": {
  "model_cpu_s0": "No for the CPU model (833,440 parameters). Fine-tuned, its quantile loss was 1.033 [1.013, 1.053] times HAR's: worse than HAR (zero-shot: 1.138 [1.102, 1.176]). The minimum-variance portfolio built on its forecasts ran at 12.2% a year against 12.1% for HAR's (+1.6%, t = 7.53): higher risk than HAR's portfolio.",
  "model_patch_s2": "The T4 model (10,083,392 parameters) has not been run: its weights are not public yet, and the protocol scores it without change when they are.",
  "described_cpu_s0": "Described after the test, not pre-registered: fine-tuned, the CPU model's quantile loss was 0.962 [0.920, 1.006] times that of the RiskMetrics average (no detectable difference), 0.882 [0.849, 0.921] times that of last month's volatility (better) and 0.748 [0.696, 0.802] times that of the 60-month mean (better). Against its own zero-shot run: 0.907 [0.877, 0.941] (better).",
  "oracle": "A perfect forecast (the oracle, which is handed next month's volatilities and cannot be invested in) would have run the same rule at 10.9% a year against 12.1% for HAR's (−11.6%, t = −21.29: lower risk). That is all the room any forecaster has with this rule.",
  "classical": "Among the classical forecasters, HAR is the standard: the closest rival, the RiskMetrics average, has a quantile loss 1.073 [1.031, 1.120] times HAR's (worse).",
  "seeds": "The CPU model's other pretraining seeds give the same verdicts, fine-tuned: seed 1: 1.034 [1.013, 1.053] times HAR's loss (worse), portfolio +0.8% against HAR's (higher risk); seed 2: 1.044 [1.024, 1.062] times HAR's loss (worse), portfolio +1.1% against HAR's (higher risk)."
 },
